{{Nayib Bukele sidebar}}

Presidential elections were held in [[El Salvador]] on 4 February 2024. Voters elected the country's [[President of El Salvador|president]] and [[Vice President of El Salvador|vice president]] to serve a five-year term. El Salvador used a [[two-round system]], and if no candidate reached an absolute majority, a second round would have occurred on 3 March 2024. The presidential election occurred concurrently with [[2024 Salvadoran legislative election|legislative elections]].

Thirteen [[List of political parties in El Salvador|political parties]] were registered with the [[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]] (TSE) making them eligible to participate in the election. Of those, only 7 parties ran candidates, of which 6 were on the ballot. Incumbent president [[Nayib Bukele]] ran for re-election with [[Nuevas Ideas]].{{efn|name="NI support"|Bukele's re-election campaign with Nuevas Ideas was supported by the [[Grand Alliance for National Unity]] (GANA),<ref name="GANABRE">{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/gana-no-presentara-candidato-a-presidente-para-apoyar-a-bukele|title=Gana No Presentará Candidato a Presidente para Apoyar a Bukele|trans-title=GANA Will Not Present a Candidate for President to Support Bukele|language=es|date=27 June 2023|access-date=27 June 2023|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Verónica|last1=Crespín|archive-date=27 June 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230627101115/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/gana-no-presentara-candidato-a-presidente-para-apoyar-a-bukele|url-status=live}}</ref> the [[Christian Democratic Party (El Salvador)|Christian Democratic Party]] (PDC),<ref name="PDCBRE">{{cite web|url=https://lanoticiasv.com/pdc-eligio-a-sus-candidatos-que-competiran-en-las-elecciones-de-2024/|title=PDC Eligió a sus Candidatos que Competirán en las Elecciones de 2024|trans-title=The PDC Elected Its Candidates Who Will Compete in the 2024 Elections|language=es|date=16 July 2023|access-date=16 July 2023|work=La Noticia SV|first1=David|last1=Morales|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador|archive-date=17 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230717064210/https://lanoticiasv.com/pdc-eligio-a-sus-candidatos-que-competiran-en-las-elecciones-de-2024/|url-status=live}}</ref> the [[National Coalition Party (El Salvador)|National Coalition Party]] (PCN),<ref name="PCNBE">{{cite web|url=https://lanoticiasv.com/partido-pcn-reitera-apoyo-a-la-candidatura-presidencial-de-nayib-bukele/|title=Partido PCN Reitera Apoyo a la Candidatura Presidencial de Nayib Bukele|trans-title=PCN Party Reiterates Support for Nayib Bukele's Presidential Candidacy|language=es|date=30 January 2024|access-date=31 January 2024|work=La Noticia SV|first1=Silvia|last1=Hernández|archive-date=31 January 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240131172904/https://lanoticiasv.com/partido-pcn-reitera-apoyo-a-la-candidatura-presidencial-de-nayib-bukele/|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Democratic Change (El Salvador)|Democratic Change]] (CD).<ref name="MGTV">{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LM4oyDzgqdc|title="Como Partido Cambio Democrático Apoyamos la Reelección del Presidente, Nayib Bukele"|trans-title="As the Democratic Change Party We Support the Re-Election of President Nayib Bukele"|language=es|date=9 November 2023|access-date=9 January 2024|work=Mágico TV|archive-date=10 January 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240110000511/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LM4oyDzgqdc|url-status=live}}</ref>}} His candidacy was controversial as the [[Constitution of El Salvador|country's constitution]] prohibited immediate re-election, but in September 2021, the [[Supreme Court of Justice of El Salvador|Supreme Court of Justice]] ruled that the incumbent president could run for immediate re-election. Various lawyers, journalists, and opposition politicians criticized Bukele's re-election bid as [[Authoritarianism|authoritarian]] and [[Constitutionality|unconstitutional]], while most Salvadorans remained highly supportive of his campaign. The other five candidates were [[Manuel Flores (Salvadoran politician)|Manuel Flores]] of the [[Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front]] (FMLN), Joel Sánchez of the [[Nationalist Republican Alliance]] (ARENA), [[Luis Parada (lawyer)|Luis Parada]] of [[Nuestro Tiempo (El Salvador)|Nuestro Tiempo]], Javier Renderos of [[Solidary Force]], [[Marina Murillo]] of the [[Salvadoran Patriotic Fraternity]] (FPS).

On election night, Bukele declared himself the winner of the election. The TSE encountered technical difficulties in the counting process and had to conduct a recount. A few days after election night, the TSE confirmed Bukele's victory. Its final results showed that Bukele won 84.65 percent of the popular vote in a [[landslide victory]]. Flores finished in second with 6.40 percent, Sánchez came in third with 5.57 percent, and the remaining candidates each received less than 5 percent. Bukele and Ulloa were inaugurated on 1 June. Bukele was the first president of El Salvador to be re-elected since Brigadier General [[Maximiliano Hernández Martínez]] in [[1944 Salvadoran presidential election|1944]].

