* {{cite web|url=https://foreignpolicy.com/2021/03/02/el-salvador-legislative-assembly-election-nuevas-ideas-nayib-bukele-fmln-arena-corruption/|title=In El Salvador, Broken Promises Have Forced the Establishment Out|language=en|date=2 March 2021|work=[[Foreign Policy]]|first1=Will|last1=Freeman|first2=Lucas|last2=Perelló|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210302173516/https://foreignpolicy.com/2021/03/02/el-salvador-legislative-assembly-election-nuevas-ideas-nayib-bukele-fmln-arena-corruption/|archive-date=2 March 2021|access-date=26 April 2023|url-status=live}}
* {{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/05/06/bukele-el-salvador-duque-colombia-protests-violence-mexico-amlo-krauze/|title=Democratic Backsliding and Unrest Add to U.S. Challenges in Latin America|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|date=6 May 2021|access-date=21 March 2023|first1=León|last1=Krauze|url-access=registration|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211217174541/https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/05/06/bukele-el-salvador-duque-colombia-protests-violence-mexico-amlo-krauze/|archive-date=17 December 2021|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="RBHZ">{{cite book|title=Exporting Autocracy: China's Role in Democratic Backsliding in Latin America and the Caribbean|language=en|date=1 February 2024|publisher=[[Center for Strategic and International Studies]]|first1=Ryan C.|last1=Berg|first2=Henry|last2=Ziemer|jstor=resrep57907|jstor-access=free|oclc=1422530372|location=[[Washington, D.C.]], United States}}</ref>{{Rp|27}} In February 2020, Bukele [[2020 Salvadoran political crisis|sent 40 soldiers into the Legislative Assembly]] to pressure lawmakers to approve a US$109 million loan to fund the [[Territorial Control Plan]], a security program initiated by Bukele, in what critics described as an attempted [[coup d'état]].<ref name="RBHZ" />{{Rp|27}}<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-51458947|title=El Salvador Parliament Denounces President's 'Attempted Coup'|date=11 February 2020|access-date=25 December 2022|work=[[BBC]]|language=en|archive-date=5 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200905170253/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-51458947|url-status=live}}</ref> In September 2020, the newspaper {{lang|es|[[El Faro (digital newspaper)|El Faro]]}} accused Bukele's government of negotiating with [[Crime in El Salvador#Gangs|criminal gangs in the country]], notably [[MS-13|Mara Salvatrucha]] (MS-13) and the [[18th Street gang]], to lower crime rates. Bukele and his government denied those accusations.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.courrierinternational.com/article/criminalite-salvador-le-president-bukele-aurait-aussi-negocie-avec-les-gangs|date=8 September 2020|title=Salvador: Le Président Bukele Aurait (Aussi) Négocié Avec les Gangs|trans-title=El Salvador: President Bukele Allegedly Negotiated with Gangs|language=fr|work=[[Courrier International]]|access-date=25 December 2022|archive-date=22 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201022070547/https://www.courrierinternational.com/article/criminalite-salvador-le-president-bukele-aurait-aussi-negocie-avec-les-gangs|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2020/09/04/nayib-bukele-niega-supuesto-pacto-con-pandillas-para-reducir-homicidios-en-el-salvador/|title=Nayib Bukele Niega Supuesto Pacto con Pandillas para Reducir Homicidios en El Salvador|trans-title=Nayib Bukele Denies Alleged Pact with Gangs to Reduce Homicides in El Salvador|date=4 September 2020|access-date=25 December 2022|first=Merlin|last=Delcid|work=[[CNN en Español]]|language=es|archive-date=16 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230216032443/https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2020/09/04/nayib-bukele-niega-supuesto-pacto-con-pandillas-para-reducir-homicidios-en-el-salvador/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the [[2021 Salvadoran legislative election|2021 legislative election]], [[Nuevas Ideas]], the political party Bukele established, won [[Supermajority|supermajorities]]<ref name="AQ">{{cite web|url=https://www.americasquarterly.org/article/in-el-salvador-a-chastened-opposition-looks-to-find-its-way/|title=In El Salvador, a Chastened Opposition Looks to Find Its Way|date=31 May 2022|access-date=8 September 2022|language=en|first1=Chase|last1=Harrison|work=[[Americas Quarterly]]|archive-date=9 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220909044217/https://www.americasquarterly.org/article/in-el-salvador-a-chastened-opposition-looks-to-find-its-way/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.ft.com/content/e59496a6-a844-425c-85ed-98341a26093e|title=El Salvador's President Seizes Control of Top Court with Firing of Judges|language=en|work=[[Financial Times]]|date=2 May 2021|access-date=1 January 2023|first1=Jude|last1=Webber|url-access=subscription|archive-date=31 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221231172153/https://www.ft.com/content/e59496a6-a844-425c-85ed-98341a26093e|url-status=live}}</ref> in the [[Legislative Assembly of El Salvador|Legislative Assembly]], [[Municipalities of El Salvador|municipalities]], and the [[Central American Parliament]] (PARLACEN).<ref name="2021L">{{cite web|url=https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/elecciones/2021/escrutinio-final/reporte/02-Asamblea-Legislativa-conformacion-departamental.pdf|url-status=live|title=Elecciones 2021 – Diputados y Diputadas para Asamblea Legislativa|trans-title=2021 Elections – Deputies for the Legislative Assembly|language=es|date=March 2021|access-date=16 September 2022|work=[[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220407043602/https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/elecciones/2021/escrutinio-final/reporte/02-Asamblea-Legislativa-conformacion-departamental.pdf|archive-date=7 April 2022}}</ref><ref name="2021M">{{cite web|url=https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/memoria-de-elecciones/2021/anexos/10-ANEXO-X-RESULTADOS-ELECCION-CONCEJOS-MUNICIPALES-2021.zip|title=Elecciones 2021 – Conformación de Concejos Municipales|trans-title=2021 Elections – Formation of Municipal Councils|language=es|date=March 2021|access-date=12 November 2022|work=[[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]]|archive-date=7 July 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220707175530/https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/memoria-de-elecciones/2021/anexos/10-ANEXO-X-RESULTADOS-ELECCION-CONCEJOS-MUNICIPALES-2021.zip|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="2021P">{{cite web|url=https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/elecciones/2021/escrutinio-final/reporte/04-Parlacen-conformacion.pdf|url-status=live|title=Elecciones 2021 – Diputados al Parlamento Centroamericano|trans-title=2021 Elections – Deputies for the Central American Parliament|language=es|date=March 2021|access-date=16 September 2022|work=[[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220121061729/https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/elecciones/2021/escrutinio-final/reporte/04-Parlacen-conformacion.pdf|archive-date=21 January 2022}}</ref> The [[List of members of the XIII Legislative Assembly of El Salvador|13th session]] of the Legislative Assembly assumed office on 1 May 2021<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/lpgdatos/Ernesto-Castro-favorito-para-dirigir-la-Asamblea-Legislativa-20210430-0095.html|title=Ernesto Castro, Favorito para Dirigir la Asamblea Legislativa|trans-title=Ernesto Castro, Favorite to Lead the Legislative Assembly|date=1 May 2021|access-date=25 December 2022|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|last=Segura|first=Edwin|language=es|archive-date=13 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513114130/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/lpgdatos/Ernesto-Castro-favorito-para-dirigir-la-Asamblea-Legislativa-20210430-0095.html|url-status=live}}</ref> and Bukele's allies [[2021 Salvadoran political crisis|voted to remove]] Attorney General [[Raúl Melara]] and five justices from the Constitutional Court of the Supreme Court of Justice. Bukele later appointed a new Attorney General and new justices in what has since been described as a [[self-coup]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/05/20/el-salvadors-president-launched-self-coup-watch-creeping-corruption-authoritarianism/|title=El Salvador's President Launched a 'Self-Coup.' Watch for Creeping Corruption and Authoritarianism.|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|date=20 May 2021|access-date=26 April 2023|language=en|first1=Manuel|last1=Meléndez-Sánchez|first2=Steven|last2=Levitsky|archive-date=6 June 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210606100412/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/05/20/el-salvadors-president-launched-self-coup-watch-creeping-corruption-authoritarianism/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Following a spike in murders in March 2022, Bukele's government began a [[Salvadoran gang crackdown|gang crackdown]], referred to as a [[state of exception]] and a war against gangs, which resulted in the reported arrests of over 75,163 alleged gang members by 11 January 2024<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diariolahuella.com/2024-inicia-garantizando-seguridad-de-salvadorenos/|title=2024 Inicia Garantizando Seguridad de Salvadoreños|trans-title=2024 Begins Guaranteeing Security of Salvadorans|language=es|date=11 January 2024|access-date=12 January 2024|work=Diario la Huella|first1=Lourdes|last1=Rodríguez|archive-date=12 January 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240112030137/https://diariolahuella.com/2024-inicia-garantizando-seguridad-de-salvadorenos/|url-status=live}}</ref> and 144 to 152 deaths in custody by 10 May 2023.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/hoy-se-aprobaria-14-prorroga-al-regimen-y-oeneges-denuncian-muertes-en-penales|title=Hoy se Aprobaría 14ª Prórroga al Régimen y Oenegés Denuncian Muertes en Penales|trans-title=Today's the 14th Extension to the State of Exception is Approved and NGOs Denounce Deaths in Prisons|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|date=16 May 2023|access-date=16 May 2023|language=es|first1=Yolanda|last1=Magaña|archive-date=16 May 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516183353/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/hoy-se-aprobaria-14-prorroga-al-regimen-y-oeneges-denuncian-muertes-en-penales|url-status=live}}</ref> The crackdown, which had since been extended several times, was accused of engaging in [[Arbitrary arrest and detention|arbitrary arrests]], [[torture]], and [[Human rights in El Salvador|human rights abuses]] by organizations such as [[Amnesty International]] and [[Human Rights Watch]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/6/2/el-salvador-committing-massive-rights-violations-amnesty-says|title=El Salvador Committing 'Massive' Rights Violations, Amnesty Says|date=2 June 2022|access-date=25 December 2022|work=[[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]]|language=en|archive-date=4 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220604171619/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/6/2/el-salvador-committing-massive-rights-violations-amnesty-says|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/02/el-salvador-evidence-serious-abuse-state-emergency|title=El Salvador: Evidence of Serious Abuse in State of Emergency|language=en|work=[[Human Rights Watch]]|date=2 May 2022|access-date=25 December 2022|archive-date=10 January 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240110151235/https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/02/el-salvador-evidence-serious-abuse-state-emergency|url-status=live}}</ref> Opposition politicians described the crackdown as a political and electoral strategy to support the government and intimidate the opposition.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/fmln-el-regimen-se-usa-para-amedrentar-la-oposicion|title=FMLN: El Régimen se Usa para Amedrentar la Oposición|trans-title=FMLN: The State of Exception is Being Used to Intimidate the Opposition|language=es|date=13 April 2023|access-date=13 April 2023|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Susana|last1=Peñate|archive-date=13 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230413180140/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/fmln-el-regimen-se-usa-para-amedrentar-la-oposicion|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Opinion polling on the Nayib Bukele presidency|Bukele remained extremely popular]] with approval ratings consistently hovering between 80 and 90{{Nbsp}}percent.<ref name="RBHZ" />{{Rp|27}}<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/world-nation/story/2021-05-16/nayib-bukele-the-most-popular-president-in-the-world-is-a-man-with-one-ideology-power|title=El Salvador's Millennial President is a Man with One Vision: Power|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|date=16 May 2021|access-date=25 December 2022|last=Linthicum|first=Kate|archive-date=26 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220926110357/https://www.latimes.com/world-nation/story/2021-05-16/nayib-bukele-the-most-popular-president-in-the-world-is-a-man-with-one-ideology-power|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://globalvoices.org/2021/02/22/despite-claims-of-authoritarianism-president-bukeles-popularity-continues-to-be-strong-in-el-salvador/|title=Despite Claims of Authoritarianism, President Bukele's Popularity Continues to be Strong in El Salvador|work=Global Voices|date=22 February 2021|last=Galdámez|first=Eddie|access-date=25 December 2022|archive-date=7 June 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210607192204/https://globalvoices.org/2021/02/22/despite-claims-of-authoritarianism-president-bukeles-popularity-continues-to-be-strong-in-el-salvador/|url-status=live}}</ref> He was considered to be one of the most popular presidents in El Salvador's history<ref>{{cite web|url=https://elsalvadorinfo.net/nayib-bukele-approval-rate/|title=Nayib Bukele Approval Rating. Majority of Salvadorans approve of Bukele|date=9 March 2022|access-date=25 March 2022|language=en|first1=Eddie|last1=Galdámez|work=El Salvador Info|archive-date=28 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220428200926/https://elsalvadorinfo.net/nayib-bukele-approval-rate/|url-status=live}}</ref> as well as one of the most popular incumbent [[Latin America]]n heads of state.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://newsinamerica.com/pdcc/boletin/2023/cid-gallup-nayib-bukele-es-el-mandatario-con-mejor-desempeno-presidencial-en-latinoamerica/|title=CID Gallup: Nayib Bukele es el Mandatario con Mejor Desempeño Presidencial en Latinoamérica|trans-title=CID Gallup: Nayib Bukele is the President with the Best Presidential Performance in Latin America|language=es|date=19 February 2023|access-date=19 February 2023|publisher=Periodico Digital – Centroamericano y del Caribe|website=News in America|archive-date=19 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230219201209/https://newsinamerica.com/pdcc/boletin/2023/cid-gallup-nayib-bukele-es-el-mandatario-con-mejor-desempeno-presidencial-en-latinoamerica/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.miamiherald.com/news/local/news-columns-blogs/andres-oppenheimer/article277719883.html|title=El Salvador's Bukele is Latin America's Most Popular President. But at What Cost?|language=en|date=29 July 2023|access-date=30 July 2023|work=[[Miami Herald]]|first1=Andres|last1=Oppenheimer|archive-date=5 February 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240205233404/https://www.miamiherald.com/news/local/news-columns-blogs/andres-oppenheimer/article277719883.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Presidential re-election controversy ===

{{see also|Continuismo}}

