On 3 September 2021, the [[Supreme Court of Justice of El Salvador|Supreme Court of Justice]] ruled that the president of El Salvador is eligible to run for re-election in consecutive terms, discarding a 2014 ruling that required presidents to wait ten years before running for re-election.<ref name="AJrel2021">{{cite web|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/9/4/el-salvador-court-says-presidents-can-serve-2-straight-terms|work=[[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]]|language=en|date=4 September 2021|access-date=5 September 2021|title=El Salvador Court says Presidents can Serve Two Straight Terms|archive-date=4 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210904192940/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/9/4/el-salvador-court-says-presidents-can-serve-2-straight-terms|url-status=live}}</ref> The court interpreted the [[Constitution of El Salvador|constitution]]'s ban on re-election as only applying to the president and not the candidates.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/felix-ulloa-se-trata-de-un-segundo-mandato-no-reeleccion|title=Félix Ulloa: "Se Trata de un Segundo Mandato", no Reelección|trans-title=Félix Ulloa: "Its a Matter of a Second Mandate", Not Re-Election|language=es|date=24 April 2023|access-date=24 April 2023|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|archive-date=24 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230424225731/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/felix-ulloa-se-trata-de-un-segundo-mandato-no-reeleccion|url-status=live}}</ref> Additionally, article 156 of the constitution prohibited anyone holding the presidency in the six months prior to the next presidential term from holding the office in the next term. The court interpreted it as permitting the president to seek re-election if they resigned or obtained a leave of office before those six months.<ref name="RBHZ" />{{Rp|29}}<ref name="LPG6months">{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Asamblea-otorga-permiso-a-Bukele-para-que-realice-campana-20231130-0091.html|title=Asamblea Otorga Permiso a Bukele para que Realice Campaña|trans-title=Assembly Grants Bukele Permission to Realize His Campaign|language=es|date=30 November 2023|access-date=30 November 2023|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Gabriel|last1=Campos Madrid|archive-date=1 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231201233327/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Asamblea-otorga-permiso-a-Bukele-para-que-realice-campana-20231130-0091.html|url-status=live}}</ref> These rulings and interpretations made Bukele eligible to run for president in 2024.<ref name="AJrel2021" /> The [[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]] (TSE) accepted the Supreme Court of Justice's ruling despite criticism from ARENA and the FMLN as well as U.S. {{lang|fr|chargé d'affaires}} to El Salvador [[Jean Elizabeth Manes]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/elections-el-salvador-9dcbdb58df7fec5b43b289c3eb269730|title=El Salvador Court Drops Ban on Presidential Re-Election|first=Marcos|last=Alemán|language=en|website=[[Associated Press]]|date=5 September 2021|access-date=5 September 2021|archive-date=6 June 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230606033203/https://apnews.com/article/elections-el-salvador-9dcbdb58df7fec5b43b289c3eb269730|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 15 September 2022, Bukele announced his re-election campaign<ref name="Henriquez">{{cite journal|last1=Henríquez|first1=Alexis|date=16 December 2024|url=https://revistas.onpe.gob.pe/index.php/elecciones/article/view/369|title=Entre la Reelección, la Transparencia y la Participación Ciudadana en el Proceso Electoral 2024 en El Salvador|trans-title=El Salvador: Between Reelection, Transparency, and Citizen Participation in the 2024 Elections|language=es|journal=Revista Elecciones|volume=23|issue=28|pages=353–374|access-date=29 July 2025|doi=10.53557/Elecciones.2024.v23n28.12|issn=1994-5272|oclc=10791999921|location=[[Mexico City]], Mexico|doi-access=free}}</ref>{{Rp|357}}<ref>{{cite web|last1=Renteria|first1=Nelson|last2=Morland|first2=Sarah|last3=Madry|first3=Kylie|date=16 September 2022|title=Despite Prohibition, El Salvador President Bukele Says he will Seek Re-Election|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/despite-prohibition-el-salvador-president-bukele-says-he-will-seek-re-election-2022-09-16/|access-date=16 September 2022|work=[[Reuters]]|language=en|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador|editor-first1=Jacqueline|editor-last1=Wong|editor-first2=Bradley|editor-last2=Perrett|archive-date=16 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220916054144/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/despite-prohibition-el-salvador-president-bukele-says-he-will-seek-re-election-2022-09-16/|url-status=live}}</ref> despite having previously voiced his opposition to immediate re-election both before and during his presidency.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/bukele-contradice-sus-propias-declaraciones/1101498/2023/|title=Bukele en 2013: "La Constitución no Permite que la Misma Persona sea Presidente Dos Veces Seguidas"|trans-title=Bukele in 2013: "The Constitution Does Not Allow the Same Person to Be President Two Consecutive Times"|language=es|date=4 November 2023|access-date=4 November 2023|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|first1=Susana|last1=Peñate|first2=Jessica|last2=García|archive-date=5 November 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231105042307/https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/bukele-contradice-sus-propias-declaraciones/1101498/2023/|url-status=live}}</ref> Bukele justified his re-election bid by arguing that most [[Developed country|developed countries]] allow re-election.<ref name="UniRel">{{cite web|url=https://www.univision.com/noticias/america-latina/nayib-bukele-anuncia-postulara-reeleccion-salvador|title=Bukele Anuncia que se Postulará para la Reelección en 2024 Pese a las Dudas Sobre su Constitucionalidad|trans-title=Bukele Announces that He Will Apply for Re-Election in 2024 Despite Doubts of Its Constitutionality|language=es|date=16 September 2022|access-date=6 May 2023|work=[[Univision]]|archive-date=19 May 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230519133719/https://www.univision.com/noticias/america-latina/nayib-bukele-anuncia-postulara-reeleccion-salvador|url-status=live}}</ref> He also claimed that restrictions on re-election only exists in [[Third World|third-world]] countries.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.telemundo52.com/noticias/mundo/el-salvador-bukele-reeleccion-presidencial-2024/2324596/|title=Presidente de El Salvador Buscará la Reelección a Pesar de Prohibición Constitucional|trans-title=President of El Salvador Seeks Re-Election Despite Constitutional Prohibition|language=es|date=16 September 2022|access-date=6 May 2023|work=[[Telemundo]]|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador|archive-date=7 May 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507012722/https://www.telemundo52.com/noticias/mundo/el-salvador-bukele-reeleccion-presidencial-2024/2324596/|url-status=live}}</ref> According to Bukele, a developed country criticizing his announcement would be hypocritical.<ref name="UniRel" /> On 25 June 2023, Bukele registered his pre-candidacy for the presidency with {{lang|es|Nuevas Ideas|italic=no}},<ref name="BULPG">{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Nuevas-Ideas-dio-a-conocer-inscripcion-de-Bukele-y-Ulloa-como-precandidatos-para-elecciones-presidenciales-20230626-0008.html|title=Nuevas Ideas Dio a Conocer Inscripción de Bukele y Ulloa como Precandidatos para Elecciones Presidenciales|trans-title=Nuevas Ideas Made It Known the Registration of Bukele and Ulloa as Pre-Candidates for the Presidential Elections|language=es|date=26 June 2023|access-date=26 June 2023|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Gabriel|last1=Campos|archive-date=10 January 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240110151145/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Nuevas-Ideas-dio-a-conocer-inscripcion-de-Bukele-y-Ulloa-como-precandidatos-para-elecciones-presidenciales-20230626-0008.html|url-status=live}}</ref> and on 9 July, {{lang|es|Nuevas Ideas|italic=no}} elected Bukele as its presidential candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/salvadoran-president-bukeles-party-names-him-2024-candidate-2023-07-10/|title=Salvadoran President Bukele's Party Names Him as 2024 Candidate|language=en|date=9 July 2023|access-date=15 July 2023|work=[[Reuters]]|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador|first1=Nelson|last1=Renteria|first2=Anna-Catherine|last2=Brigida|editor1-first=Tom|editor1-last=Hogue|archive-date=10 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230710122601/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/salvadoran-president-bukeles-party-names-him-2024-candidate-2023-07-10/|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:2023 Salvadoran protests – "No a la Reelección" banner.png|thumb|right|alt=Street protesters holding a white banner with red text reading "No to Re-Election" in Spanish|Protesters holding a banner reading "No to Re-Election"]]

Bukele's announcement that he was running for re-election was criticized by constitutional lawyers who argued his re-election would be [[Constitutionality|unconstitutional]] and violate at least four articles of El Salvador's constitution.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/world-nation/story/2022-09-16/el-salvador-presidents-re-election-bid-draws-strong-reaction|title=El Salvador President's Re-election Bid Despite Constitutional Ban Draws Strong Reaction|language=en|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|first1=Marcos|last1=Alemán|date=16 September 2022|access-date=16 September 2022|archive-date=16 March 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230316041959/https://www.latimes.com/world-nation/story/2022-09-16/el-salvador-presidents-re-election-bid-draws-strong-reaction|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Constitucionalista-reitera-prohibicion-de-reeleccion-en-El-Salvador-20230215-0099.html|title=Constitucionalista Reitera Prohibición de Reelección en El Salvador|trans-title=Constitutionalist Reiterates the Prohibition of Re-election in El Salvador|language=es|date=16 February 2023|access-date=17 February 2023|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Lissette|last1=Mondragón|archive-date=10 January 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240110220715/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Constitucionalista-reitera-prohibicion-de-reeleccion-en-El-Salvador-20230215-0099.html|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Manuel Flores (Salvadoran politician)|Manuel Flores]], then the FMLN's presidential pre-candidate, stated that "the law is the law [and] the law says that there is no re-election" ("{{lang|es|la ley es la ley [y] la ley dice que no hay reelección}}"), adding that the constitution is "clear" ("{{lang|es|clara}}") in prohibiting re-election.<ref name="MFrel">{{cite web|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/elecciones-manuel-flores-fmln-precandidato/1071135/2023/|title=Manuel Flores, Precandidato del FMLN, le Recuerda a Bukele que la Reelección es "Inconstitucional"|trans-title=Manuel Flores, Pre-Candidate of the FMLN, Reminds Bukele that Re-Election is "Unconstitutional"|language=es|date=26 June 2023|access-date=26 June 2023|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|first1=Lissette|last1=Lemus|archive-date=26 June 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230626154042/https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/elecciones-manuel-flores-fmln-precandidato/1071135/2023/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[2023 Salvadoran protests|Protest marches]] against Bukele's government and against his re-election bid were held in San Salvador on 1 May and 15 September 2023. Flores, Joel Sánchez, and [[Luis Parada (lawyer)|Luis Parada]] (the presidential candidates of the FMLN, ARENA, and {{lang|es|[[Nuestro Tiempo (El Salvador)|Nuestro Tiempo]]|italic=no}}, respectively) participated in the latter protest march.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/siete-organizaciones-de-izquierda-anuncian-marcha-contra-regimen-y-reeleccion|title=Siete Organizaciones de Izquierda Anuncian Marcha Contra Régimen y Reelección|trans-title=Seven Leftist Organizations Announce March Against State of Exception and Re-Election|language=es|date=12 April 2023|access-date=12 April 2023|first1=Yolanda|last1=Magaña|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|archive-date=12 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230412220219/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/siete-organizaciones-de-izquierda-anuncian-marcha-contra-regimen-y-reeleccion|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="15pro">{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/medicos-candidatos-presidenciales-y-diputados-en-marcha-contra-reeleccion-falta-de-medicinas-y-capturas-de-inocentes|title=Médicos, Candidatos Presidenciales y Diputados en Marcha Contra Reelección, Falta de Medicinas y Capturas de Inocentes|trans-title=Doctors, Presidential Candidates and Deputies March Against Re-Election, Lack of Medicine, and the Capture of Innocents|language=es|date=15 September 2023|access-date=15 September 2023|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Verónica|last1=Crespín|archive-date=15 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230915222221/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/medicos-candidatos-presidenciales-y-diputados-en-marcha-contra-reeleccion-falta-de-medicinas-y-capturas-de-inocentes|url-status=live}}</ref>

In October and November 2023, {{lang|es|Nuestro Tiempo|italic=no}}, ARENA, and various NGOs filed ten requests with the TSE calling for the body to not register Bukele's presidential candidacy or void his candidacy after he initiated the registration process.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Presentan-una-10-peticion-de-nulidad-de-la-candidatura-de-Nayib-Bukele-20231107-0080.html|title=Presentan una 10ª Petición de Nulidad de la Candidatura de Nayib Bukele|trans-title=They Presented the 10th Petition to Nullify Nayib Bukele's Candidacy|language=es|date=8 November 2023|access-date=8 November 2023|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Lissette|last1=Mondragón|archive-date=23 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231223002117/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Presentan-una-10-peticion-de-nulidad-de-la-candidatura-de-Nayib-Bukele-20231107-0080.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On 9 November, the TSE reaffirmed that Bukele's candidacy was legal and dismissed all the petitions to void his candidacy.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/TSE-dice-que-resolucion-sobre-candidatura-de-Bukele-esta-en-firme-20231109-0097.html|title=TSE Dice que Resolución Sobre Candidatura de Nayib Bukele en Elecciones 2024 Está en Firme|trans-title=TSE Says the Resolution Regarding Nayib Bukele in the 2024 Elections is Firm|language=es|date=10 November 2023|access-date=11 November 2023|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Gabriela|last1=Villarroel|archive-date=5 February 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240205233355/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/TSE-dice-que-resolucion-sobre-candidatura-de-Bukele-esta-en-firme-20231109-0097.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 30 November, to abide by article 156 of the constitution, Bukele was granted a leave of absence by the Legislative Assembly. [[Claudia Rodríguez de Guevara]], Bukele's private secretary, was named as the [[President of El Salvador#Succession|presidential designate]].<ref name="LPG6months" />{{efn|In El Salvador, a "designate" ("{{lang|es|designado}}") is someone appointed by the Legislative Assembly to be next in the line of succession to the presidency after the president and vice president. The Legislative Assembly can appoint up to two designates.<ref>{{cite book|editor1-last=Haggerty|editor1-first=Richard A.|date=1990|title=El Salvador: A Country Study|url=https://archive.org/details/elsalvadorcountr00hagg|language=en|edition=2nd|location=[[Washington, D.C.]], United States|publisher=[[Federal Research Division]]|isbn=9780525560371|lccn=89048948|oclc=1044677008|access-date=7 July 2025}}</ref>{{Rp|152}}}} She assumed office the following day.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.swissinfo.ch/spa/el-salvador-elecciones_secretaria-privada-de-bukele-ocupar%C3%A1-la-presidencia-durante-licencia-del-mandatario/49021872|title=Secretaria Privada de Bukele Ocupará la Presidencia Durante Licencia del Mandatario|trans-title=Private Secretary of Bukele Will Occupy the Presidency During Leave of Absence of the Incumbent|language=es|date=1 December 2023|access-date=2 December 2023|work=Swiss Info|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador|archive-date=2 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231202102644/https://www.swissinfo.ch/spa/el-salvador-elecciones_secretaria-privada-de-bukele-ocupar%C3%A1-la-presidencia-durante-licencia-del-mandatario/49021872|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://gatoencerrado.news/2023/12/01/sustituye-ilegalmente-a-bukele-en-los-ultimos-seis-meses-de-su-presidencia/|title=Claudia Juana Rodríguez Sustituye Ilegalmente a Bukele en los Últimos Seis Meses de su Presidencia|trans-title=Claudia Juana Rodríguez Illegally Substitutes Bukele in the Final Six Months of His Presidency|language=es|date=1 December 2023|access-date=1 December 2023|work=Gato Encerrado|first1=Eugenia|last1=Velásquez|first2=Beatriz|last2=Benítez|archive-date=1 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231201234422/https://gatoencerrado.news/2023/12/01/sustituye-ilegalmente-a-bukele-en-los-ultimos-seis-meses-de-su-presidencia/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/world-news/general-news-e03108ba9371398ad529cf44b8259d1f|title=La Nueva Presidenta Interina de El Salvador Era Hasta Ayer la Secretaria Privada de Bukele|trans-title=The New Interim President of El Salvador Was Until Yesterday the Private Secretary of Bukele|language=es|date=1 December 2023|access-date=1 December 2023|work=[[Associated Press]]|archive-date=2 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231202220548/https://apnews.com/world-news/general-news-e03108ba9371398ad529cf44b8259d1f|url-status=live}}</ref> Rodríguez was the first woman in Salvadoran history to hold presidential powers, although she never held the office of President.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.infobae.com/america/agencias/2023/12/01/secretaria-privada-de-bukele-ocupara-la-presidencia-durante-licencia-del-mandatario/|title=Secretaria Privada de Bukele Ocupará la Presidencia Durante Licencia del Mandatario|trans-title=Bukele's Private Secretary Will Occupy the Presidency During the Leave of Absence of the Incumbent|language=es|date=30 November 2023|access-date=7 July 2025|work=[[Infobae]]|archive-date=2 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231202045440/https://www.infobae.com/america/agencias/2023/12/01/secretaria-privada-de-bukele-ocupara-la-presidencia-durante-licencia-del-mandatario/|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Electoral system ==

=== Election procedure ===

A presidential election was held in El Salvador five years after the 2019 presidential election.<ref name="AQ" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.swissinfo.ch/spa/el-salvador-elecciones_el-salvador-necesita-una-agenda-de-reformas-electorales--dice-eurodiputado/47066778|title=El Salvador Necesita una Agenda de Reformas Electorales, Dice Eurodiputado|trans-title=El Salvador Needs an Agenda for Electoral Reforms, Says European Deputy|date=28 October 2021|access-date=12 November 2021|language=es|website=swissinfo.ch|archive-date=13 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211113050937/https://www.swissinfo.ch/spa/el-salvador-elecciones_el-salvador-necesita-una-agenda-de-reformas-electorales--dice-eurodiputado/47066778|url-status=live}}</ref> The president and vice president were elected through a [[Direct election|popular vote]] and El Salvador's constitution mandated that the election was "free, direct, equal and [[Secret ballot|secret]]".<ref name="constitution">{{cite web|url=http://confinder.richmond.edu/admin/docs/ElSalvador1983English.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150103200933/http://confinder.richmond.edu/admin/docs/ElSalvador1983English.pdf|date=15 December 1983|archive-date=3 January 2015|access-date=12 November 2021|title=Constitution of El Salvador|language=en|work=[[University of Richmond]]|pages=Articles 78–80|url-status=dead}}</ref> The election was held 4 February 2024, concurrent with [[2024 Salvadoran legislative election|legislative elections]]. If a second round had been necessary, it would have occurred on 3 March.<ref name="ElectionDate">{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/bbda3951657a87a8772b735acd6e9421|title=El Salvador Fija Elección Presidencial para Febrero 2024|trans-title=El Salvador Fixes Presidential Election for February 2024|language=es|work=[[Associated Press]]|date=11 August 2022|access-date=16 November 2022|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador|archive-date=17 November 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221117015304/https://apnews.com/article/bbda3951657a87a8772b735acd6e9421|url-status=live}}</ref> To win the election, a candidate needed an [[Majority|absolute majority]] (50%{{Nbsp}}+{{Nbsp}}1). If no candidate received an absolute majority, a [[Two-round system|second round]] between the two candidates with the most valid votes would have occurred within 30 days of the first round.<ref name="constitution" /><ref name="Nohlen">{{cite book|last1=Krennerich|first1=Michael|chapter=El Salvador|date=2005|editor-last1=Nohlen|editor-first1=Dieter|editor1-link=Dieter Nohlen|title=Elections in the Americas A Data Handbook Volume 1: North America, Central America, and the Caribbean|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-xZREAAAQBAJ|language=en|location=[[Oxford]], United Kingdom|publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]|pages=270–299|isbn=9780191557934|oclc=58051010|access-date=9 January 2023}}</ref>{{Rp|272–274}} Presidential and vice presidential candidates had to be at least 30 years old and be Salvadoran citizens by birth.<ref name="Aspire">{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/partidos-aspiran-a-tener-mas-alcaldes-y-diputados-en-2024|title=Partidos Aspiran a Tener Más Alcaldes y Diputados en 2024|trans-title=Parties Aspire to Have More Mayors and Deputies in 2024|date=12 December 2022|access-date=2 January 2023|first1=Verónica|last1=Crespín|language=es|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|archive-date=2 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230102184007/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/partidos-aspiran-a-tener-mas-alcaldes-y-diputados-en-2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Electronic voting]] for Salvadoran expatriates began at midnight on 6 January 2024.<ref name="ExpatriateVoters">{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/arranca-voto-electronico-para-741094-salvadorenos-con-dui-en-el-exterior|title=Arranca Voto Electrónico para 741,094 Salvadoreños con DUI en el Exterior|trans-title=Electronic Voting for 741,094 Salvadorans with DUI in the Exterior Begins|language=es|date=6 January 2024|access-date=6 January 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Verónica|last1=Crespín|archive-date=6 January 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240106135530/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/arranca-voto-electronico-para-741094-salvadorenos-con-dui-en-el-exterior|url-status=live}}</ref>{{efn|On 18 October 2022, the Legislative Assembly passed the Special Law for the Exercise of Suffrage in the Exterior that allowed [[Non-resident citizen voting|Salvadorans living outside of the country]] to vote electronically in the presidential and [[2024 Salvadoran legislative election|concurrent legislative elections]].<ref name="Henriquez" />{{Rp|359}}<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/asamblea-aprueba-voto-electronico-en-el-exterior-para-presidenciales-y-legislativas-de-2024|title=Asamblea Aprueba Voto Electrónico en el Exterior para Presidenciales y Legislativas de 2024|trans-title=Assembly Approves Electronic Voting in the Exterior for Presidential and Legislative Elections in 2024|language=es|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|date=18 October 2022|access-date=2 January 2023|archive-date=2 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230102194616/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/asamblea-aprueba-voto-electronico-en-el-exterior-para-presidenciales-y-legislativas-de-2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/nueva-ley-de-voto-en-exterior-no-aplicara-para-parlacen-y-concejos-municipales|title=Nueva Ley de Voto en Exterior no Aplicará para Parlacen y Concejos Municipales|trans-title=New Law of the Exterior Vote does not Apply to PARLACEN or Municipal Councils|language=es|date=17 October 2022|access-date=2 January 2023|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Yolanda|last1=Magaña|archive-date=2 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230102201029/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/nueva-ley-de-voto-en-exterior-no-aplicara-para-parlacen-y-concejos-municipales|url-status=live}}</ref>}} [[Right of Salvadoran expatriates to vote|Expatriates were able to vote]] at 81 voting centers in 60 of the [[List of diplomatic missions of El Salvador|country's embassies and consulates]] in 30 countries.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/tse-preve-217-maquinas-de-votacion-electronica-en-81-centros-de-60-ciudades|title=TSE Prevé 217 Máquinas de Votación Electrónica en 81 Centros de 60 Ciudades|trans-title=TSE Foresees 217 Electronic Voting Machines in 81 Centers in 60 Cities|language=es|date=21 August 2023|access-date=21 August 2023|first1=Verónica|last1=Crespín|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|archive-date=21 August 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230821213330/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/tse-preve-217-maquinas-de-votacion-electronica-en-81-centros-de-60-ciudades|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Salvadorenos-en-22-paises-no-podran-ejercer-el-voto-de-forma-presencial-en-proximas-elecciones-20230803-0069.html|title=Salvadoreños en 22 Países no Podrán Ejercer el Voto de Forma Presencial en Próximas Elecciones|trans-title=Salvadorans in 22 Countries Will Not Be Able to Vote in Person in Upcoming Elections|language=es|date=4 August 2023|access-date=4 August 2023|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Gabriela|last1=Villarroel|archive-date=8 August 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230808203655/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Salvadorenos-en-22-paises-no-podran-ejercer-el-voto-de-forma-presencial-en-proximas-elecciones-20230803-0069.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Voting in El Salvador was held from 7 a.m. to 5 p.m. [[Central Time Zone|CST]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diariolahuella.com/centros-de-votacion-abiertos-de-700-a-m-a-500-p-m/|title=Centros de Votación Abiertos de 7:00 a.m a 5:00 p.m.|trans-title=Voting Centers Open from 7:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.|language=es|date=4 February 2024|access-date=4 February 2024|work=Diario la Huella|archive-date=4 February 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240204043304/https://diariolahuella.com/centros-de-votacion-abiertos-de-700-a-m-a-500-p-m/|url-status=live}}</ref> A total of 1,595 voting centers were available across the country.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/tse-aun-sin-definir-la-cantidad-de-centros-de-votacion-para-las-elecciones-de-2024|title=TSE Aún Sin Definir la Cantidad de Centros de Votación para las Elecciones de 2024|trans-title=TSE Yet to Define the Quantity of Voting Centers for the 2024 Elections|language=es|date=10 October 2023|access-date=10 October 2023|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Verónica|last1=Crespín|archive-date=10 October 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231010184010/https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/tse-aun-sin-definir-la-cantidad-de-centros-de-votacion-para-las-elecciones-de-2024|url-status=live}}</ref> Voting was not compulsory.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-el-salvadors-2024-presidential-and-legislative-elections|title=Explainer: El Salvador's 2024 Presidential and Legislative Elections|language=en|date=31 January 2024|access-date=6 February 2024|work=[[Americas Society|AS/COA]]|first1=Chase|last1=Harrison|archive-date=6 February 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240206212644/https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-el-salvadors-2024-presidential-and-legislative-elections|url-status=live}}</ref> There were a total of 6,214,399 registered voters.<ref name="Henriquez" />{{Rp|364}} Guillermo Wellman, a magistrate of the TSE, stated that individuals arrested during the country's gang crackdown were ineligible to vote.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Rodríguez|first1=Milton|date=1 March 2023|title=Los Capturados por el Régimen de Excepción Están "Impedidos" de Votar en el 2024: Magistrado del TSE|trans-title=Those Captured during the State of Exception Are "Not Allowed" to Vote in 2024: Magistrate of the TSE|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/capturados-regimen-excepcion-impedidos-votar-2024-tse/1043527/2023/|access-date=1 March 2023|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|language=es|archive-date=1 March 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230301224349/https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/capturados-regimen-excepcion-impedidos-votar-2024-tse/1043527/2023/|url-status=live}}</ref>

