== Aftermath ==

=== Domestic reactions ===

[[File:Nayib Bukele's supporters in front of the National Palace (4 February 2024).png|thumb|right|alt=A crowd of Nayib Bukele's supporters gathered in front of the National Palace awaiting for Bukele give a speech following his victory in the 2024 presidential election.|A crowd of Bukele's supporters in front of the [[National Palace (El Salvador)|National Palace]] on election night]]

Shortly after polling stations closed but before the TSE officially announced the results of the election, Bukele declared on [[Twitter|X]] that, "according to our numbers", he had won the presidential election with "more than 85% of the votes".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/el-salvadors-bukele-declares-himself-winner-presidential-vote-2024-02-05/|title=El Salvador's President Bukele Declares Himself Winner of Presidential Vote|language=en|date=4 February 2024|access-date=4 February 2024|work=[[Reuters]]|first1=Nelson|last1=Renteria|first2=Sarah|last2=Kionsian|first3=Drazen|last3=Jorgic|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref> He also claimed that his electoral victory was "a record in the entire democratic history of the world"<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20240205-el-salvador-s-president-bukele-claims-record-reelection-victory|title=El Salvador's President Bukele Claims "Record" Re-Election Victory|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[France 24]]}}</ref> and that the 2024 election was "the first time in history that El Salvador has democracy, and it's not me saying it, it's the people".<ref name="FTresults">{{cite web|url=https://www.ft.com/content/1306b424-b250-48c5-a383-6dedac67f482|title=El Salvador's President Nayib Bukele Set for Landslide Election Victory after Gang Crackdown|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[Financial Times]]|first1=Christine|last1=Murray|url-access=subscription}}</ref> A crowd of Bukele's supporters gathered at the Plaza Gerardo Barrios in San Salvador to celebrate his victory. Bukele later addressed the crowd from the balcony of the [[National Palace (El Salvador)|National Palace]] and celebrated his victory in a speech,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/el-salvador-election-bukele-gang-violence-democracy-dc9f65f450875c5841f937753cd3101a|title=Salvadorans Celebrating Expected Re-Election of "World's Coolest Dictator" as Their President|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[Associated Press]]|first1=Megan|last1=Janetsky|first2=Marcos|last2=Alemán|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/elecciones-el-salvador-tse/1120969/2024/|title=En Vivo: Nayib Bukele con Amplia Ventaja con el 31% de Actas Procesadas, Según el TSE|trans-title=Live: Nayib Bukele with High Support at 31% of Votes Processed, According to the TSE|language=es|date=4 February 2024|access-date=4 February 2024|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|first1=Xiomara|last1=Alfaro}}</ref> during which he criticized journalists who themselves had criticized the gang crackdown and his concentration of power.<ref name="FTresults" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://thehill.com/policy/international/ap-el-salvadors-nayib-bukele-heads-for-reelection-as-president-buoyed-by-support-for-gang-crackdown/|title=El Salvador's Nayib Bukele Takes Aim at Critics in Looking Ahead to 2nd Presidential Term|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|first1=Megan|last1=Janetsky|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref> Bukele also remarked that the election results was "the first time in a country that just one party exists in a completely democratic system", adding that "the entire opposition together was pulverized".<ref name="APresults">{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/el-salvador-nayib-bukele-president-reelection-ef04e20d901908099f4f787b841aca89|title=El Salvador's Nayib Bukele Heads for Re-Election as President but Troubled Scrutiny Delays Results|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[Associated Press]] |first1=Megan |last1=Janetsky |first2=Marcos |last2=Alemán |location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref>

Former FMLN Salvadoran president [[Mauricio Funes]] stated that the opposition was defeated "without a doubt" ("{{lang|es|no cabe ninguna duda}}").<ref name="LNSVMF">{{cite web|url=https://lanoticiasv.com/funes-sin-ninguna-duda-la-oposicion-fue-derrotada-claudia-ortiz-seguira-dando-la-batalla/|title=Funes: Sin Ninguna Duda, la Oposición fue Derrotada. "Claudia Ortiz Seguirá Dando la Batalla"|trans-title=Funes: Without a Doubt, the Opposition was Defeated. "Claudia Ortiz Will Continue Fighting the Battle"|language=es|date=20 February 2024|access-date=21 February 2024|work=La Noticia SV|first1=David|last1=Morales|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref> In a press conference on 4 February 2024, Flores stated that the FMLN would not disappear as a political entity and that it was "not correct" ("{{lang|es|no es correcto}}") for Bukele to declare himself the election's winner before the TSE made an official announcement. Óscar Ortiz thanked those who voted for Flores and stated that "history does not end here" ("{{lang|es|la historia no termina acá}}").<ref>{{cite web|url=https://lanoticiasv.com/manuel-flores-no-desaparecimos-pero-si-desaprecio-el-tribunal-supremo-electoral/|title=Manuel Flores: "No Desaparecimos, pero Sí Desapreció el Tribunal Supremo Electoral"|trans-title=Manuel Flores: "We Will Not Disappear, but the Supreme Electoral Court Did Disappear"|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=La Noticia SV|first1=Griselda|last1=López|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref> Flores did not concede and later claimed that he had votes stolen from him.<ref name="FDNC">{{cite web|url=https://lanoticiasv.com/manuel-flores-del-fmln-no-asume-la-derrota-si-me-han-robado-votos/|title=Manuel Flores del FMLN no Asume la Derrota: "Si me han Robado Votos"|trans-title=Manuel Flores of the FMLN Does Not Concede Defeat: "They Have Stolen Votes from Me"|language=es|date=19 February 2024|access-date=20 February 2024|work=La Noticia SV|first1=David|last1=Morales|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref> On 5 February, Claudia Ortiz stated that "democratic spaces are closing in El Salvador, civil society is closing down and there is an environment of fear to speak out".<ref name="Rres1">{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/bukele-landslide-shifts-el-salvador-one-party-state-amplifies-democracy-fears-2024-02-05/|title=Bukele Landslide Shifts El Salvador to One-Party State, Amplifies Democracy Fears|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[Reuters]]|first1=Sarah|last1=Kinosian|first2=Nelson|last2=Renteria|editor1-first=Drazen|editor1-last=Jorgic|editor2-first=Christian|editor2-last=Plumb|editor3-first=Rosalba|editor3-last=O'Brien}}</ref> On 7 February, Sánchez conceded the election and congratulated Bukele on his victory.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/joel-sanchez-retorna-a-su-actividad-empresarial-y-le-desea-sabiduria-a-bukele|title=Joel Sánchez Retorna a su Actividad Empresarial y le Desea Sabiduría a Bukele|trans-title=Joel Sánchez Returns to His Business Activities and Wishes Wisdom to Bukele|language=es|date=7 February 2024|access-date=7 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]}}</ref>

=== International reactions ===

[[Bernardo Arévalo]], the [[president of Guatemala]];<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/presidente-de-guatemala-felicita-a-bukele-por-su-eventual-reeleccion|title=Presidente de Guatemala Felicita a Bukele por su Eventual Reelección|trans-title=President of Guatemala Congratulates Bukele on His Eventual Re-Election|language=es|date=4 February 2024|access-date=4 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Walter|last1=Solórzano}}</ref> [[Xiomara Castro]], the [[president of Honduras]];<ref name="LPGreactions">{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Paises-de-la-region-felicitan-a-Nayib-Bukele-antes-de-que-se-conozcan-los-resultados-de-eleccion-presidencial-20240204-0086.html|title=Países de la Región Felicitan a Nayib Bukele, Antes de que se Conozcan los Resultados de Elección Presidencial|trans-title=Countries of the Region Congratulate Nayib Bukele, Before the Results of the Presidential Election are Known|language=es|date=4 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Denni|last1=Portillo|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240205233418/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Paises-de-la-region-felicitan-a-Nayib-Bukele-antes-de-que-se-conozcan-los-resultados-de-eleccion-presidencial-20240204-0086.html|archive-date=5 February 2024|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Daniel Ortega]], the [[president of Nicaragua]];<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Daniel-Ortega-felicita-a-hermano-Nayib-Bukele-por-su-reeleccion-20240205-0060.html|title=Daniel Ortega Felicita a "Hermano Nayib Bukele" por su Reelección|trans-title=Daniel Ortega Congratulates "Brother Nayib Bukele" on His Re-Election|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=6 February 2024|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Denni|last1=Portillo}}</ref> [[Laurentino Cortizo]], the [[president of Panama]];<ref name="LPGreactions" /> [[Miguel Díaz-Canel]], the [[First Secretary of the Communist Party of Cuba|first secretary of the Communist Party of Cuba]]; [[Xi Jinping]], the [[General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party|general secretary of the Chinese Communist Party]];<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/internacional/Cuba-y-China-felicitan-a-Nayib-Bukele-por-su-reeleccion-en-El-Salvador-20240207-0011.html|title=Cuba y China Felicitan a Nayib Bukele por su Reelección en El Salvador|trans-title=Cuba and China Congratulate Nayib Bukele on His Re-Election in El Salvador|language=es|date=7 February 2024|access-date=7 February 2024|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]}}</ref> [[Vladimir Putin]], the [[president of Russia]];<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/presidente-de-rusia-felicita-a-nayib-bukele-por-su-reeleccion-y-espera-que-prosiga-la-cooperacion-rusosalvadorena|title=Presidente de Rusia Felicita a Nayib Bukele por Su Reelección y Espera que Prosiga la "Cooperación Ruso–Salvadoreña"|trans-title=President of Russia Congratulates Nayib Bukele on His Re-Election and Awaits that "Ruso-Salvadoran Cooperation" Continues|language=es|date=12 February 2024|access-date=12 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Javier|last1=Maldonado}}</ref> and [[Alicia Bárcena Ibarra]], the [[Secretary of Foreign Affairs (Mexico)|secretary of foreign affairs of Mexico]], congratulated Bukele on X after he declared himself as the election's winner. The Chilean and Chinese embassies in El Salvador also congratulated Bukele before the release of the official results,<ref name="LPGreactions" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/2/4/el-salvador-votes-with-nayib-bukele-poised-for-second-presidential-term|title=El Salvador's Bukele Claims Election Win Before Official Results Announced|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|archive-date=5 February 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240205020126/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/2/4/el-salvador-votes-with-nayib-bukele-poised-for-second-presidential-term|url-status=live}}</ref> as did the United Nations.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/onu-felicita-a-bukele-por-su-reelecion|title=ONU Felicita a Bukele por su Reelección|trans-title=UN Congratulates Bukele on His Re-Election|language=es|date=7 February 2024|access-date=7 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]}}</ref> The [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs, International Trade and Worship]] of Argentina congratulated Bukele and reaffirmed commitment for both countries to work together.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.infobae.com/america/agencias/2024/02/05/argentina-felicita-a-bukele-y-afirma-compromiso-de-continuar-trabajando-con-el-salvador/|title=Argentina Felicita a Bukele y Afirma "Compromiso de Continuar Trabajando" con El Salvador|trans-title=Argentina Congratulates Bukele and Reaffirms "Commitment to Continue Working" with El Salvador|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=6 February 2024|work=[[Infobae]]|location=[[Buenos Aires]], Argentina}}</ref>
<!--Don't add reactions without a secondary source; ie, don't use Twitter as a primary source-->

[[United States Secretary of State]] [[Antony Blinken]] congratulated Bukele on X, stating that "the events in El Salvador have a direct impact on the interests of the United States" and that the American and Salvadoran governments could "reach our maximum potential and surpass major obstacles in our hemisphere and on a global level" through cooperation. [[William H. Duncan]], the [[List of ambassadors of the United States to El Salvador|United States ambassador to El Salvador]], congratulated Bukele, adding that he hoped to "continue working with representatives of the Salvadoran people elected to fortify democracy, increase economic opportunities, and confront the shared challenges of the 21st century".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/eeuu-felicita-a-bukele-y-dice-que-espera-con-interes-trabajar-con-el|title=EEUU Felicita a Bukele y Dice que Espera con Interés Trabajar con Él|trans-title=US Congratulates Bukele and Says They Wait with Interest to Work with Him|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]}}</ref> Several members of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] in the [[United States Senate]] congratulated Bukele's victory, but expressed that they were "troubled" regarding "unconstitutional moves that strongly influenced the outcome of Sunday's election" and statements made by Ulloa regarding "'eliminating' and 'replacing' democracy".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://thehill.com/policy/international/4448950-senate-democrats-troubled-by-unconstitutional-moves-that-affected-el-salvador-election/|title=Senate Democrats Troubled by "Unconstitutional Moves" that Affected El Salvador Election|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|first1=Sarah|last1=Fortinsky}}</ref> [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] senators [[Ted Cruz]] and [[Marco Rubio]] also congratulated Bukele.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/senadores-de-florida-y-texas-felicitan-a-bukele-y-piden-su-apoyo-para-mantener-el-orden-democratico-y-la-paz-en-la-region|title=Senadores de Florida y Texas Felicitan a Bukele y Piden su Apoyo para Mantener el "Orden Democrático y la Paz en la Región"|trans-title=Senators from Florida and Texas Congratulate Bukele and Ask for His Support in Maintaining "Democratic Order and Peace in the Region"|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Javier|last1=Maldonado}}</ref>

On 5 February 2024, several foreign news publications called the presidential election for Bukele, describing it as a "[[landslide victory]]".<ref name="FTresults" /><ref>Multiple sources:
* {{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-68196826|title=El Salvador's President Bukele Wins Re-Election by Huge Margin|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[BBC]]}}
* {{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/02/04/world/americas/election-el-salvador-bukele.html|title=El Salvador's President Claims Election Victory in a Landslide|language=en|date=4 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[The New York Times]]|first1=Gabriel|last1=Labrador|first2=Natalie|last2=Kitroeff|location=[[San Salvador]] and [[Mexico City]]}}
* {{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/el-salvador-president-bukele-poised-another-landslide-voters-head-polls-2024-02-04/|title=El Salvador's Bukele Re-Elected as President in Landslide Win|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[Reuters]]|first1=Nelson|last1=Renteria|first2=Sarah|last2=Kinosian|first3=Diego|last3=Ore|first4=Natalia|last4=Siniawski|first5=Drazen|last5=Jorgic|editor1-first=Will|editor1-last=Dunham|editor2-first=Chizu|editor2-last=Nomiyama|editor3-first=Nick|editor3-last=Zieminski|editor4-first=Sonali|editor4-last=Paul|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}
* {{cite web|url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/coolest-dictator-philosopher-king-nayib-bukeles-path-reelection-106968042|title="Coolest Dictator" to "Philosopher King," Nayib Bukele's Path to Re-Election in El Salvador|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]|first1=Christopher|last1=Sherman|location=[[Mexico City]], Mexico}}
* {{cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/02/05/americas/el-salvador-election-results-intl/index.html|title=El Salvador's Nayib Bukele Set for Landslide Election Victory|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[CNN]]|first1=David|last1=Shortell|first2=Stefano|last2=Pozzebon|first3=Martin|last3=Goillandeau}}
* {{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/digital-assets/2024/02/05/el-salvador-embraces-future-with-bitcoin-as-bukele-secures-historic-victory/?sh=33c926557efc|title=El Salvador Embraces Future With Bitcoin As Bukele Secures Historic Victory|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[Forbes]]|first1=Susie Violet|last1=Ward}}</ref> Foreign news publications also described {{lang|es|Nuevas Ideas|italic=no}}' concurrent legislative victory, in which the party won 54 out of 60 seats, as creating a "{{lang|la|[[de facto]]}} one-party state".<ref name="Rres1" /><ref name="WPres1">{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2024/02/06/bukele-nayib-el-salvador-president-coolest-dictator-global-international/|title=The Inescapable Appeal of the World's "Coolest Dictator"|language=en|date=6 February 2024|access-date=6 February 2024|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|first1=Ishaan|last1=Tharoor}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://english.elpais.com/international/2024-02-06/bukele-expands-his-power-in-el-salvador-the-era-of-the-single-party-and-the-single-leader-is-born.html|title=Bukele Expands His Power in El Salvador: The Era of the Single Party and the Single Leader is Born|language=en|date=6 February 2024|access-date=6 February 2024|work=[[El País]]|first1=Juan Diego|last1=Quesada|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref> Tyler Mattiace, a researcher for [[Human Rights Watch]], described the election as a "growing rejection of the basic principles of democracy and human rights, and support for authoritarian populism among people who feel that, concepts like democracy and human rights and due process have failed them".<ref name="WPres1" />

=== Allegations of irregularities ===

Shortly after polling stations closed at 5 p.m. CST, Bukele accused the TSE and Indra Solutions of preventing expatriates from casting their vote by not allowing them to cast their vote after the stated time. He argued that people waiting in line before the polling stations closed still had to be allowed to vote and called upon Attorney General [[Rodolfo Delgado (lawyer)|Rodolfo Delgado]] to launch an investigation. Delgado responded by stating that the office of the attorney general "will prosecute those who committed a crime".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/bukele-acusa-a-tse-e-indra-de-cerrar-centros-de-votacion-en-exterior-e-impedir-voto-de-diaspora|title=Bukele Acusa a TSE e Indra de Cerrar Centros de Votación en Exterior e Impedir Voto de Diáspora|trans-title=Bukele Accuses the TSE and Indra of Closing Voting Centers in the Exterior and Impeding the Diaspora Vote|language=es|date=4 February 2024|access-date=4 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Javier|last1=Maldonado}}</ref> The TSE announced that it would re-open voting at three voting centers in the United States that had not allowed Salvadorans to vote after 5 p.m. CST, however, the TSE later retraced its announcement following a petition made by ARENA.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/TSE-da-marcha-atras-a-reabrir-voto-presencial-en-el-exterior-20240206-0104.html|title=TSE da Marcha Atrás a Reabrir Voto Presencial en el Exterior|trans-title=TSE Walks Back Re-Opening In-Person Voting in the Exterior|language=es|date=7 February 2024|access-date=7 February 2024|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Denni|last1=Portillo}}</ref> Bukele responded on X to the TSE's retraction by stating "remember this, when the opposition asks you again for votes in the next elections".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diariolahuella.com/presidente-bukele-recuerden-esto-cuando-la-oposicion-vuelva-a-pedirles-el-voto-en-las-proximas-elecciones/|title=Presidente Bukele: "Recuerden Esto, Cuando la Oposición Vuelva a Pedirles el Voto en las Próximas Elecciones"|trans-title=President Bukele: "Remember This, When the Opposition Asks You Again for Votes in the Next Elections"|language=es|date=6 February 2024|access-date=7 February 2024|work=Diario la Huella|first1=Édgar|last1=Torres}}</ref>

The TSE released the first preliminary results late on 4 February 2024 but stalled until the morning of 5 February with its website crashing shortly before midnight local time. The TSE attributed the delay to "multiple actions that have hampered the development of the transmission activities of preliminary results" and shortages of paper to print out vote tallies. It advised electoral boards at polling stations to resort to contingency measures such as tallying votes by hand and taking photographs or scans of manual tallies before sending them to the TSE.<ref name="APresults" /> Deputies from ARENA, the FMLN, {{lang|es|Vamos|italic=no}}, and {{lang|es|Nuestro Tiempo|italic=no}} criticized the TSE's counting delay.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/partidos-de-oposicion-se-quejan-de-la-tardanza-de-resultados-del-escrutinio-final|title=Partidos de Oposición se Quejan de la Tardanza de Resultados del Escrutinio Final|trans-title=Opposition Parties Complain of the Delay of the Final Scrutiny Results|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Magdalena|last1=Reyes}}</ref> {{lang|es|Nuestro Tiempo|italic=no}} claimed that there were inconsistencies between preliminary vote totals published by the TSE in published statements and on its website.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/nuestro-tiempo-senala-inconsistencias-en-resultados-preliminares-divulgados-por-el-tse|title=Nuestro Tiempo Señala Inconsistencias en Resultados Preliminares Divulgados por el TSE|trans-title=Nuestro Tiempo Signals Inconsistencies in Preliminary Results Published by the TSE|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Verónica|last1=Crespín}}</ref> Óscar Picardo, the director of sciences at the Francisco Gavidia University, stated that "we've never seen a situation that affects electoral integrity like this ... it's obvious that Bukele has the most votes, as the polls say, but there are a lot of irregularities in the processing".<ref name="FTresults" />

ARENA, the FMLN, {{lang|es|Vamos|italic=no}}, and {{lang|es|Nuestro Tiempo|italic=no}} all called for the TSE to launch a [[election recount|recount]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://lanoticiasv.com/oposicion-pide-recuento-voto-por-voto-ante-irregularidades-en-el-sistema-de-transmision-de-datos/|title=Oposición Pide Recuento "Voto por Voto" ante Irregularidades en el Sistema de Transmisión de Datos|trans-title=Opposition Asks for Recount "Vote by Vote" after Irregularities in the Data Transmission System|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=La Noticia SV|first1=Verónica|last1=Martínez|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref> On 5 February 2024, the TSE stated that it would conduct a recount of 29 percent of the presidential ballots at the [[Gimnasio Nacional José Adolfo Pineda|José Adolfo Pineda National Gymnasium]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/tse-contara-voto-por-voto-para-definir-diputados-por-fallas-en-la-transmision|title=TSE Contará Voto por Voto para Definir Diputados, por Fallas en la Transmisión|trans-title=TSE Will Count Vote by Vote to Define Deputies, due to Faults in the Transmission|language=es|date=6 February 2024|access-date=6 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Yolanda|last1=Magaña}}</ref> and that the results would be finalized within 15 days.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/tse-tendria-resultados-oficiales-dentro-de-dos-semanas-dice-magistrado/1121533/2024/|title=TSE Tendría Resultados Oficiales Dentro de Dos Semanas, Dice Magistrado|trans-title=TSE Will Have Official Results Within Two Weeks, Says Magistrate|language=es|date=6 February 2024|access-date=6 February 2024|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|first1=Jessica|last1=García}}</ref> The recount began on 7 February<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/tse-inicia-proceso-de-escrutinio-final-y-alista-recuento-de-votos-2024|title=TSE Inicia Proceso de Eccrutinio Final y Alista Recuento de Votos 2024|trans-title=TSE Begins Process of Final Results and Begins Recount of Votes|language=es|date=7 February 2024|access-date=7 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Yolanda|last1=Magaña}}</ref> and ended on 9 February.<ref name="Final Results P" /> A similar recount occurred for the concurrent legislative election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/tse-inicia-escrutinio-de-votos-legislativos-arena-se-retira|title=TSE Inicia Escrutinio de Votos Legislativos, ARENA se Retira|trans-title=TSE Initiates Scrutiny for Legislative Votes, ARENA Resigns|language=es|date=12 February 2024|access-date=12 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Jaqueline|last1=Villeda}}</ref>

The [[Organization of American States]] (OAS) sent a election observation mission led by [[Isabel Saint Malo]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/oea-nombro-expresidenta-panama-observacion-elecciones/1116184/2024/|title=OEA Nombró a Exvicepresidenta de Panamá Jefa de Observación para las Elecciones 2024|trans-title=OAS Named the Former Vice President of Panama as the Head of Observation for the 2024 Elections|language=es|date=12 January 2024|access-date=15 January 2024|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|first1=Abigail|last1=Parada|archive-date=5 February 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240205233245/https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/oea-nombro-expresidenta-panama-observacion-elecciones/1116184/2024/|url-status=live}}</ref> Although the OAS voiced concern to the TSE during the recount process regarding the TSE leaving the recount process "in the hands of the representatives of the political parties",<ref name="APSPM">{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/el-salvador-nayib-bukele-election-8637667ca3b9f35c9ffd2baf805a9ade|title=El Salvador's Bukele Wins Supermajority in Congress after Painstaking Vote Count|language=en|date=19 February 2024|access-date=20 February 2024|work=[[Associated Press]]|first1=Marcos|last1=Alemán|first2=Megan|last2=Janetsky|location=[[San Salvador]] and [[Mexico City]]}}</ref> the OAS stated that "the Mission did not observe actions that would indicate that the will of the citizenry expressed at the polls was altered" ("{{lang|es|la Misión no observó hechos que indicaran que se alteró la voluntad de la ciudadanía expresada en las urnas}}").<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/OEA-vuelve-a-cuestionar-falta-de-control-del-TSE-sobre-escrutinio-final-20240222-0030.html|title=OEA Vuelve a Cuestionar Falta de Control del TSE Sobre Escrutinio Final|trans-title=OAS Again Questions the TSE's Lack of Control Regarding the Final Scrutiny|language=es|date=22 February 2024|access-date=22 February 2024|work=[[La Prensa Gráfica]]|first1=Denni|last1=Portillo}}</ref> The Interchange and Solidarity Center stated that it had observed irregularities at 69 voting centers both in El Salvador and in the United States.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/elecciones-2024-observadores-irregularidades/1121284/2024/|title=Observadores Internacionales del CIS Señalan Irregularidades en Elecciones|trans-title=International Observers of the CIS Signal Irregularities in Elections|language=es|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|first1=Lissette|last1=Lemus}}</ref> Eduardo Escobar, a lawyer for the Citizen Action non-governmental organization, stated that there were "surely" irregularities and questioned how much of an impact they had on the final results.<ref name="APSPM" />

=== Presidential transition ===

[[File:Nayib Bukele y Gabriela de Bukele en Palacio Nacional.jpg|thumb|right|alt=A photograph of Nayib Bukele and Gabriela Rodríguez waving from a balcony|Bukele with his wife, [[Gabriela Rodríguez de Bukele|Gabriela Rodríguez]], at his second inauguration]]

The TSE ratified the results of the presidential election on 18 February 2024.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2024/02/18/el-salvador-tribunal-supremo-electoral-acta-escrutinio-final-elecciones-orix/|title=El Salvador: Tribunal Supremo Electoral Emite Notificación Sobre Acta de Escrutinio Final de las Elecciones Presidenciales|trans-title=El Salvador: Supreme Electoral Court Issues Notification Regarding the Final Scrutiny of the Presidential Election|language=es|date=18 February 2024|access-date=3 March 2024|work=[[CNN en Español]]}}</ref> It granted Bukele and Ulloa their presidential and vice presidential credentials, respectively, on 29 February.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://diario.elmundo.sv/politica/tse-entrega-credenciales-a-bukele-y-ulloa-para-periodo-presidencial-2024-2029|title=TSE Entrega Credenciales a Bukele y Ulloa para Período Presidencial 2024-2029|trans-title=The TSE Gives Credentials to Bukele and Ulloa for the 2024–2029 Presidential Term|language=es|date=29 February 2024|access-date=29 February 2024|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Verónica|last1=Crespín}}</ref> Bukele and Ulloa [[Nayib Bukele#Inaugurations|were inaugurated]] for a second term on 1 June at the Plaza Gerardo Barrios in [[San Salvador Historic Downtown|San Salvador's historic downtown]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/bukele-anuncia-medicina-amarga-para-resolver-economia/1146268/2024/|title=Nayib Bukele Asume Segundo Periodo y Anuncia "Medicina Amarga" para Resolver Economía|trans-title=Nayib Bukele Assumes Second Term and Announces "Bitter Medicine" to Resolve the Economy|language=es|date=1 June 2024|access-date=12 June 2024|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|first1=Susana|last1=Peñate}}</ref>

Alexis Henríquez, a writer for the Observatory of Political Reforms in Latin America, described the 2024 election as a "watershed" for the Salvadoran political party system due to reforms that enabled Bukele's re-election.<ref name="Henriquez" />{{Rp|355}} Bukele is the first person since Brigadier General [[Maximiliano Hernández Martínez]] (in office 1931–1934 and 1935–1944) to be re-elected and serve multiple terms as president.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://elfaro.net/en/202309/el_salvador/27055/Reelection-in-El-Salvador-Rhymes-with-Dictatorship.htm|title=La Reelección en El Salvador es Sinónimo de Dictadura|trans-title=Re-Election in El Salvador Rhymes with Dictatorship|language=es|date=19 November 2021|access-date=19 September 2023|work=[[El Faro (digital newspaper)|El Faro]]|first1=Nelson|last1=Rauda Zablah|archive-date=18 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230918125020/https://elfaro.net/en/202309/el_salvador/27055/Reelection-in-El-Salvador-Rhymes-with-Dictatorship.htm|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://nacla.org/el-salvador-slide-toward-authoritarianism|title=El Salvador's Slide Toward Authoritarianism|last1=Ng|first1=Johnathan|language=en|work=[[North American Congress on Latin America]]|date=2 November 2022|access-date=4 November 2022|archive-date=3 November 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221103155224/https://nacla.org/el-salvador-slide-toward-authoritarianism|url-status=live}}</ref> Additionally, he was the first president since [[Antonio Saca]] (served 2004–2009) to seek re-election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.brookings.edu/opinions/reelection-continuity-and-hyper-presidentialism-in-latin-america/|title=Re-Election, Continuity and Hyper-Presidentialism in Latin America|language=en|work=[[Brookings Institution]]|first1=Daniel|last1=Zovatto|date=12 February 2014|access-date=26 April 2023|archive-date=26 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230426185912/https://www.brookings.edu/opinions/reelection-continuity-and-hyper-presidentialism-in-latin-america/|url-status=live}}</ref>{{efn|Antonio Saca's re-election bid was for the [[2014 Salvadoran presidential election|2014 presidential election]], one election cycle after his initial election in [[2004 Salvadoran presidential election|2004]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-presidential-reelection-latin-america|title=Explainer: Presidential Re-Election in Latin America|date=28 April 2015|access-date=19 May 2023|first1=Rachel|last1=Glickhouse|work=[[Americas Society|AS/COA]]|archive-date=20 May 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230520010634/https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-presidential-reelection-latin-america|url-status=live}}</ref>}}

Immediately after Bukele's re-election victory, ''[[The Economist]]'' indicated that Bukele may potentially seek to change the constitution and run for a third presidential term in the [[2027 Salvadoran presidential election|next presidential election]], as he had previously stated that the constitution did not "currently" allow for a third presidential term.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.economist.com/the-americas/2024/02/05/after-nayib-bukeles-crushing-unconstitutional-victory-what-next|title=After Nayib Bukele's Crushing, Unconstitutional Victory, What Next?|language=en|date=5 February 2024|access-date=5 February 2024|newspaper=[[The Economist]]|location=[[Izalco]] and [[San Salvador]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240205232748/https://www.economist.com/the-americas/2024/02/05/after-nayib-bukeles-crushing-unconstitutional-victory-what-next|archive-date=5 February 2024|url-status=live|url-access=subscription}}</ref> In a July 2024 interview with ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'', Bukele stated that he would not seek a third term in 2029.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/nayib-bukele-elecciones-presidenciales-el-salvador-entrevista-revista-time-/1165082/2024/|title=Nayib Bukele a Revista Time: "No Puedo Correr para Presidente de Nuevo"|trans-title=Nayib Bukele to Time Magazine: "I Cannot Run for President Again"|language=es|date=29 August 2024|access-date=4 September 2024|work=[[El Diario de Hoy]]|first1=Abigail|last1=Parada|first2=Carlos|last2=López Vides}}</ref>{{efn|This interview occurred before July 2025 when the [[Legislative Assembly of El Salvador|Legislative Assembly]] voted to move the date of the next presidential election from 2029 to 2027.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/czd04q87zryo|title=El Salvador Scraps Term Limits, Paving Way for Bukele to Rule Indefinitely|language=en|date=1 August 2025|access-date=1 August 2025|work=[[BBC]]|first1=Chris|last1=Graham}}</ref>}}

